WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.692 --> 00:00:14.900
STEPHANIE WONG: Hi, everyone.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.239
Welcome to Eyes on Enterprise.

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:17.697
I'm Stephanie Wong,
and today we're

00:00:17.697 --> 00:00:20.495
talking with Brian Dorsey,
Developer Advocate for Compute.

00:00:20.495 --> 00:00:22.120
And we're going to
be talking about how

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.180
to approach cloud
infrastructure for enterprises

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.790
and how to decide where
to deploy your workloads.

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:30.640
We have a multitude of
options on Google Cloud.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.920
And so it can be a
challenge to optimize

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.530
for things like scalability,
performance, developer

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:36.453
experience.

00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:38.120
So Brian, thanks so
much for being here.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.170
BRIAN DORSEY: Thanks
so much for having me.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.060
This is one of my favorite
topics, so I'm really excited.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:41.770
STEPHANIE WONG:
That's great to hear.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.960
So first off, there
are lots of options

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.420
on Compute, Compute Engine,
App Engine, and Cloud Run.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.457
And enterprises, they have
a lot of legacy systems

00:00:49.457 --> 00:00:51.790
and a lot of dependencies in
place like message brokers,

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.008
databases, web apps.

00:00:53.008 --> 00:00:54.550
So through the lens
of an enterprise,

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.560
how should they make
that initial decision?

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:57.090
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:57.637
Yeah.

00:00:57.637 --> 00:00:58.470
It gets complicated.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:00:59.860
We have a lot of
different options

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.485
and enterprises are
complicated things.

00:01:01.485 --> 00:01:03.007
So I think--

00:01:03.007 --> 00:01:05.340
I like to think about it as
kind of an abstraction, kind

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.540
of a layer cake almost,
where at the bottom

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.130
you have things
like VMs, and then

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.270
you kind of move up and
up in layers as you go.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.030
So you've got things
like Kubernetes,

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.405
or Cloud Run, App
Engine functions,

00:01:15.405 --> 00:01:16.780
and they get more
abstract as you

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.218
get kind of closer to the top.

00:01:18.218 --> 00:01:20.260
And I think enterprises,
also they're big, right?

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:24.370
So you end up often running
multiples of those things

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.510
together to solve the
same-- in the same system.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.000
STEPHANIE WONG: To be honest,
I think a lot of enterprises

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.042
face this challenge of
feeling like they're

00:01:30.042 --> 00:01:31.750
going to make the
wrong decision and they

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:34.270
need to make the right
decision from the get-go.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.695
So how do you help them make
that choice to dive in and not

00:01:38.695 --> 00:01:40.570
feel like they have
wasted time and resources

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.520
investing in the wrong
infrastructure option?

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:42.820
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:43.940
That can be really scary, right?

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.648
You know, like all
this effort and if you

00:01:45.648 --> 00:01:47.420
have to change things later.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.170
So I think there's a
few things we can do.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:50.910
You can kind of try
to lower the risk,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.990
and there's some technical
things we can do that.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.120
I think running
software in containers

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.290
really helps with that.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.460
Nearly all of our
computer environments

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:00.710
support running software
that's running in containers.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.960
I think that's true for most
of the other environments

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.320
people might be using.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.710
So I think that helps a lot.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.456
Also, you can just decide
not to move it, right?

00:02:08.456 --> 00:02:10.539
You know, you've got
[INAUDIBLE] existing systems.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:11.935
Maybe, you know--

00:02:11.935 --> 00:02:14.120
I think one of my favorite
definitions of enterprise

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.440
is where most of the software
doesn't have an engineering

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.400
team on it at all.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:19.817
You know it needs
to keep running.

00:02:19.817 --> 00:02:20.540
It needs to work.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.230
But it's the ops folks who are
like trying to keep that going.

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:26.590
And there I think VMs
are really useful.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.320
And once you have some of your
software running in cloud,

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.710
you're trying to make a
bigger system out of it.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.060
So you started trying
to glue it together.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:33.610
And I think that's where
serverless and things like

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:34.420
that can help.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:34.660
STEPHANIE WONG: OK.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:34.820
Wait.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:35.110
Hold on.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:35.610
Serverless.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.730
So everyone has a different
definition of serverless.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.460
Even at Google, we
have varying products

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.300
under the serverless ecosystem.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.858
So help us define what
that is here in this case.

00:02:45.858 --> 00:02:47.400
BRIAN DORSEY: I'm
not going to do it.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.642
Like, there's so many
definitions as serverless.

00:02:49.642 --> 00:02:50.850
I think it's a little tricky.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.290
But for the purpose
of this conversation,

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.320
let's focus on that kind of
pay-for-use, like the actual--

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.768
you do some work and you
pay for only that work.

00:02:59.768 --> 00:03:01.810
I think that's the core
definition of serverless.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.180
And I think it will help
in this conversation.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:03.690
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.300
I mean, wouldn't you argue that
that pay-for-use model applies

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:07.592
to Compute Engine, too, though?

00:03:07.592 --> 00:03:08.520
BRIAN DORSEY: Kind of.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:09.937
So when we're
talking pay-for-use,

00:03:09.937 --> 00:03:12.660
we're thinking of the
actual work being done.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.490
And Compute Engine, and our
VMs, and several other things

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:17.100
in cloud, you're
kind of reserved--

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.020
you're paying while
it's on, virtually.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.640
So you're paying
for the reservation.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.765
And you might not be getting
any value out of that.

00:03:22.765 --> 00:03:24.372
You might just be
sitting there idle.

00:03:24.372 --> 00:03:26.330
So there are things you
can do to minimize that

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:27.190
and you want to do that.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:28.740
But the serverless
stuff is designed

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:31.100
so you're only paying for
the actual work being done.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:31.975
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:03:31.975 --> 00:03:32.490
OK.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.290
BRIAN DORSEY: You know, give
an HTTP request, or responding

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.760
to a message broker,
or something like that.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.060
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.320
So it's more event driven.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.140
You have the potential to scale
down to zero, things like that.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:40.950
BRIAN DORSEY: Exactly that.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:41.390
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:41.890
OK.

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:44.770
So let's just talk about these
abstraction layers in order,

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:45.870
if you don't mind
going through them.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:46.260
BRIAN DORSEY: Totally.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:47.927
You have kind of
functions as a service,

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:50.720
and I think there
is Cloud Functions.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.830
And you know, from
a developer-- this

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.755
is a developer tool primarily.

00:03:54.755 --> 00:03:56.130
From the developer's
perspective,

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.430
you write this like small
piece of code, a function,

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:02.100
and you kind of push
it up to Google Cloud.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.530
And we do all of the
work of running it.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.690
It responds to an event,
like you said earlier,

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.810
or that might be
something coming from HTTP

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.083
or from a message system.

00:04:11.083 --> 00:04:13.500
Does a little bit of work,
maybe a side effect, maybe not,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.060
and then returns.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:15.560
That's it.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.727
STEPHANIE WONG: So what
makes serverless a good fit?

00:04:17.727 --> 00:04:19.522
And when would it
be something that's

00:04:19.522 --> 00:04:20.855
more of a constraint for people?

00:04:20.855 --> 00:04:23.660
BRIAN DORSEY: So it's showing
up in lots and lots of spaces.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.970
So I think people are
exploring all the use cases.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:27.238
So consider that.

00:04:27.238 --> 00:04:29.280
But some of the places
that are especially strong

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.130
is in kind of gluing
systems together,

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.380
where you've got a bunch
of existing pieces.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.797
And you're trying
to make them all

00:04:35.797 --> 00:04:37.860
behave like they're one system.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.110
And also in a lot of,
kind of, message flow kind

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.990
of scenarios where people
are doing either data

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.270
transformations, or
they're moving information

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.398
from one system to another.

00:04:46.398 --> 00:04:47.940
Things like that is
where it shows up

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.370
the most for this level
of the abstraction, that

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:51.420
function side.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.840
STEPHANIE WONG: Right, and
then besides Cloud Functions

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.580
and Cloud Run, we also have
our longtime managed service

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.093
from 2007, App Engine.

00:04:58.093 --> 00:04:59.760
And that's been great
for developing web

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.110
applications and the like.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.730
So can you explain
what App Engine is

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.230
and what it's used
for specifically?

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:05.810
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah
so App Engine,

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:07.070
it's a wider view of the code.

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.130
You have a full code base.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.560
It's still a code-focused
kind of environment.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.640
And you push that
whole code base.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:18.360
It's really meant for
hosting web apps primarily.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.595
And so you have
the different URLs

00:05:20.595 --> 00:05:22.987
respond to to and the different
code that handles that.

00:05:22.987 --> 00:05:24.070
And it works as one piece.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:27.450
So you've got the code,
hosted database pieces,

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.100
and the same kind of idea.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.200
You have an app, you
push it to Google,

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:32.760
it runs it, scales
it up and down.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.970
It's really good for things
where it's really bursty,

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:38.070
where you can't really
plan for the usage,

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.990
and you need to be able
to respond, but also

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:41.100
not have a bunch
of infrastructure

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:42.910
sitting there idle when
you're not using it.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:44.010
STEPHANIE WONG: Right,
and I can imagine

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.320
with retail online
shops that are expecting

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.805
huge, bursting events like
Black Friday, things like that.

00:05:48.805 --> 00:05:49.930
BRIAN DORSEY: Exactly that.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:51.030
STEPHANIE WONG: All
right, so working our way

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.850
down the abstraction layer
cake, we have Cloud Run.

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.508
Can you tell me
about what that is?

00:05:55.508 --> 00:05:56.550
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.520
So it's kind of trying
to make the combination

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.740
of the containers world
and the serverless world,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.323
and give you kind
of the best of both.

00:06:03.323 --> 00:06:04.980
So from the container
side, it lets

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:07.800
you run any software that
will run in a container

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.090
and can listen to a web
request, HTTP request, or events

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:14.028
coming that same
way, and serverless.

00:06:14.028 --> 00:06:16.320
You get to pay for what you
use, and only what you use.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.140
You get this hosted environment
to run anything you want,

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:19.930
basically.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:20.490
STEPHANIE WONG: So
Cloud Run, yeah.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.740
It seems like it's something
that developers have been

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.410
rallying for for a while now.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.910
They want the portability and
the flexibility of containers,

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:29.190
but they also want the agility
that serverless gives them.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:29.970
BRIAN DORSEY: Absolutely.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:31.050
STEPHANIE WONG: So we've
mentioned containers

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:32.310
a bunch of times already.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:33.210
What is it exactly?

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:34.495
Let's talk about that.

00:06:34.495 --> 00:06:36.370
BRIAN DORSEY: So from
a technical standpoint,

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:37.260
there's a bunch of
things you could say.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.430
We're usually talking
about Docker containers.

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.580
Under the covers,
they're really, actually,

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.360
a [INAUDIBLE],, a ZIP
file of some files.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.320
Practically, though, I think
what it does is it solves the--

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.390
works on "my machine"
problem, like where

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:52.890
something worked
in one environment,

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:53.910
but doesn't in another.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.410
That worked on my machine.

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.742
Like, so it just makes
things much more consistent

00:06:57.742 --> 00:06:58.950
wherever you're running them.

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:00.908
I think that's the key
takeaway for containers.

00:07:00.908 --> 00:07:02.783
STEPHANIE WONG: So for
those that are already

00:07:02.783 --> 00:07:04.470
running a large
number of containers,

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:06.563
what would you recommend
them to deploy on?

00:07:06.563 --> 00:07:07.980
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah,
I mean I think

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.470
that's what Kubernetes
was invented to help with.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:14.500
So it's basically a
service, a system,

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.850
a set of tools, open source
environment for running lots

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:20.940
of containers across
lots of machines,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.600
but with the idea of treating--

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.600
instead of thinking about
individual containers,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.460
we're thinking about
individual computers.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:27.930
You're thinking about the
application as a whole,

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:29.888
or like microservices as
you mentioned earlier,

00:07:29.888 --> 00:07:32.010
and how they fit together,
and how the pieces talk

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:35.528
to each other, and managing
all of that communication

00:07:35.528 --> 00:07:37.070
as well as the
containers themselves,

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.953
and insulating you from the
actual, physical or virtual

00:07:39.953 --> 00:07:41.120
hardware they're running on.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.037
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:07:42.037 --> 00:07:45.260
So that brings us to GKE,
or Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:47.805
Tell us more about
what that is used for.

00:07:47.805 --> 00:07:49.180
BRIAN DORSEY:
Yeah, so Kubernetes

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:50.375
is an open source project.

00:07:50.375 --> 00:07:53.000
And it helps that this runs in
a lot of different environments.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.880
And Google Kubernetes Engine
is taking the expertise

00:07:55.880 --> 00:08:01.610
that Google engineers and
SREs built, building this up

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:04.130
and the longtime experience
of running software at Google

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:06.560
to make a hosted
version of Kubernetes,

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.130
where we handle the
upgrades to the software,

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:11.870
keeping the masters
online, things

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:14.990
like that, and basically, let
you focus on the application

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.032
even more than you would.

00:08:17.032 --> 00:08:18.740
And under the covers,
it's running on VMs

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.350
and the rest of Google Cloud.

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:21.990
But you don't have
to worry about that.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:23.240
It's just kind of all handled.

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:23.690
It's up.

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.260
If you want to make
it larger or smaller,

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:27.450
there's knobs you can turn
to do that sort of thing.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:27.830
STEPHANIE WONG:
Is there any kind

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:29.540
of opportunity for
those that are running

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:32.780
their workloads on App Engine to
move over to Kubernetes Engine,

00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:33.367
for example?

00:08:33.367 --> 00:08:34.159
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:39.200
And in fact, basically all
up and down this stack,

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:40.760
most of the
environments allow you

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:42.240
to run software in containers.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:47.670
So that's true from App
Engine, Cloud Run, Kubernetes,

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:50.035
and VM's compute engine
we talk about in a minute.

00:08:50.035 --> 00:08:51.410
So once you get
into a container,

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:53.060
you can move things
around pretty smoothly.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.602
STEPHANIE WONG: All
right, so on GKE,

00:08:54.602 --> 00:08:56.450
what are good use
cases for, and what

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:57.800
are some of the constraints?

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:00.050
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah, so it
makes this insulation layer.

00:09:00.050 --> 00:09:03.050
So that is a good
example, so if you

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.300
need to run the same application
in multiple different

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:06.810
environments.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.020
So this comes up, for
like dev and test and prod

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:10.160
for almost everybody.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:14.370
But you can also see it come
up for writing game servers,

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:15.800
for example.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:18.290
You need to build to run in
different legal environments.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:19.940
We're talking
enterprises here, right?

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:23.840
And large companies have
legal-specific policies.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:26.240
They have teams that are
responsible for different parts

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:28.370
of the systems.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:30.140
You need to be able
to plug your system

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:31.250
into the other things
that are already

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:32.330
going on the enterprise.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:34.430
And Kubernetes is
especially good at that.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:35.390
It's really modular.

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:38.240
And you can connect your
different enterprise pieces

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:39.920
into every level,
from authentication

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:43.562
to policy about firewalls, all
the way up and down the stacks,

00:09:43.562 --> 00:09:45.020
and get the different
teams who are

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:47.240
responsible for those
involved in working

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:48.803
at it at the Kubernetes level.

00:09:48.803 --> 00:09:50.720
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
and just for performance

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:53.480
and also just optimization
for utilization

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:55.862
of your compute resources,
how does it affect that?

00:09:55.862 --> 00:09:57.445
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah,
so the utilization

00:09:57.445 --> 00:09:59.810
is the key phrase there.

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:02.227
That's the reason everybody
cares about containers at all,

00:10:02.227 --> 00:10:04.310
so you can run more software
on the same machines.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:05.360
STEPHANIE WONG: Exactly.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:07.402
BRIAN DORSEY: And that's
what containers are for,

00:10:07.402 --> 00:10:10.040
and honestly why
Google cared about it.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:12.230
You get more running
on same hardware.

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:14.660
And the same thing is
true in cloud as a whole.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:19.010
In cloud, utilization
usage costs money.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:22.700
So if you can optimize
there, it saves you money

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:24.020
and hopefully time as well.

00:10:24.020 --> 00:10:26.062
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, so
we just talked about GKE

00:10:26.062 --> 00:10:27.680
and some of the
performance and cost

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:29.138
optimizations that
you get with it.

00:10:29.138 --> 00:10:31.700
But, I mean, I want to talk a
little bit about that low level

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:34.100
of abstraction, Compute Engine.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.550
It gives you a lot
of flexibility.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:37.460
You can take care of
everything that you

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:40.537
deploy on the VM, like
software and any installations

00:10:40.537 --> 00:10:41.370
that you have on it.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:43.203
So when does it actually
makes sense for you

00:10:43.203 --> 00:10:44.410
to choose Compute Engine?

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:45.750
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah, so this is--

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.380
it's one of my favorite services
because it underlies so much

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:49.255
of the rest of cloud.

00:10:49.255 --> 00:10:52.160
For one, GKE is,
in turn, running

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:53.630
on top of Compute Engine.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:56.240
Our managed databases are.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:59.120
And I think that's especially
relevant for enterprises

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:00.835
because you have
so much software

00:11:00.835 --> 00:11:02.960
that doesn't have an
engineering team on it, right?

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.180
You need to keep it working.

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:09.500
And almost always, the best
first step for that is to--

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:11.080
it may already be
running in a VM,

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:14.540
and if not, to move it into a
VM or move it into a Cloud VM.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:15.860
And you can usually do that.

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:17.402
It's flexible enough,
you can usually

00:11:17.402 --> 00:11:20.990
do that without having to change
hardly anything because it

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:21.765
is a computer.

00:11:21.765 --> 00:11:22.890
It's a virtualized machine.

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:24.320
So you can run the
same things you've

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:25.440
been running other places.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:26.540
STEPHANIE WONG: So
you mentioned software

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:28.873
that needs to run without a
developer team supporting it

00:11:28.873 --> 00:11:30.510
necessarily all the time.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:33.290
So give me some examples there,
like any stories that you have.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:34.248
BRIAN DORSEY: Oh, yeah.

00:11:34.248 --> 00:11:37.490
I mean, I think this is like
classic enterprise, right?

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:39.012
You know, you've got--

00:11:39.012 --> 00:11:40.470
you've got the team
that builds it.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:41.960
And then they have to move
on and build the next thing

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:43.290
and build the next thing
and build the next thing.

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:45.260
So even in-house
software tends to not

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:47.120
have active engineering on it.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:49.598
But also you're
buying or renting

00:11:49.598 --> 00:11:51.140
software from other
places that needs

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:54.210
to work third party software
you have to keep running.

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:56.600
And then there may just be
some old stuff where you don't

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:58.460
know the history of it at all.

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:02.190
There's that thing that
nobody quite knows how to redo

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:04.310
but needs to keep working.

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:06.860
And all of those, I think, are
a good fit for running on VMs.

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:07.340
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:08.780
Yeah, I think we've all
have been in that case

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:10.197
where we're working
with software,

00:12:10.197 --> 00:12:12.295
but the code base is so old.

00:12:12.295 --> 00:12:13.670
It takes so long
to make changes.

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:16.150
And maybe it's not the
best fit for deploying

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:18.212
in a containerized
environment right now.

00:12:18.212 --> 00:12:19.670
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah,
so for anything

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:26.483
where it's not the right time
to kind of make changes to it,

00:12:26.483 --> 00:12:27.900
the VMs are a good
place to start.

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:29.210
STEPHANIE WONG: So we just went
through the abstraction layer

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:30.440
cake, as you mentioned.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:33.200
And there are a ton of
options we just covered.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.180
So, once again, to kind of
wrap our heads around this,

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:37.710
how do you make that decision?

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:39.793
BRIAN DORSEY: Part of it
is just this abstraction,

00:12:39.793 --> 00:12:42.460
like how do you want to
be working as a team?

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:44.160
And if your team is
mostly developers,

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:45.970
that's going to keep you kind
of higher at the abstraction.

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:48.420
If you're mostly kind of
ops or have deep integration

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:50.340
requirements or
existing things, you're

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:52.300
mostly going to be
on the lower levels.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.150
There's also some
technical constraints.

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:55.858
It's worth checking
the docs to make sure

00:12:55.858 --> 00:12:57.420
that these specific
things you need

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.962
are available in each
of the environment.

00:12:59.962 --> 00:13:01.920
And then I kind of
mentioned team a little bit.

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:03.390
But I just want to
double down on that.

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:04.710
What is your team
most comfortable with?

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:06.300
What do you care about the most?

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:09.070
What do you need to do
and want to do as a team?

00:13:09.070 --> 00:13:12.180
And I think that may also
kind of affect things there.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:14.670
The other piece is
maybe not so obvious

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:17.100
is just where in
the lifecycle it is.

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:20.910
For things that are newer, you
have the full range of options.

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:24.130
And I encourage people to start
high on the abstraction thing

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:25.630
and go down if you need to.

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:27.130
For existing systems,
you're usually

00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:31.420
going to be working your way
up from other VMs or containers

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:32.440
kind of from the bottom.

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:33.030
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:34.170
All right, so we
talked about so much.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:35.400
But what can people
do to actually

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:36.620
get started on some of these?

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:37.140
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:39.090
I think, yeah, there
are a lot of options.

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:42.050
And I think if specific
ones of these jumped out,

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:44.462
and you're like, oh,
that's what I need, go dig

00:13:44.462 --> 00:13:45.420
into that a little bit.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:47.118
Look at the docs,
we have code labs

00:13:47.118 --> 00:13:49.410
on each of these for what it
feels like to actually use

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:51.210
and get started on.

00:13:51.210 --> 00:13:53.760
If you want a bit
more information,

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:55.650
I did a much longer
talk on the same topic.

00:13:55.650 --> 00:14:00.290
And you can take a look at
that and kind of go from there.

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:01.290
But yeah, give it a try.

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:04.050
Think about how this works
for your applications,

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:06.660
and what your challenges are,
and where adding some of this

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:07.980
in is going to really help out.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:09.300
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, I
think the important point

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:10.680
that you made earlier
was you don't actually

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:12.180
have to move all your
stuff to the cloud.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.340
You can pick and choose the
ones that make sense and then

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:15.750
think about which
layer of abstraction

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.760
makes the most sense for
your development teams.

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:18.468
All right, Brian.

00:14:18.468 --> 00:14:20.260
Thank you so much for
being on the show.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:21.480
I encourage everyone
to check out

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:23.688
Brian's talk where he dives
in much deeper about some

00:14:23.688 --> 00:14:24.550
of these topics.

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:27.330
And again, join us next
time for Eyes on Enterprise.

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:30.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

